## Solving the system

Collecting the terms of (13.2) that multiply $u_{ij}$ and moving
the rest to the right gives the Gauss--Seidel update used by the multigrid
relaxation ([chapter 16](16_multigrid)):
$$
u_{ij} \leftarrow \frac{r_{ij}\Delta^2 + \frac{\Delta t}{\rho}\,N_{ij}}
{\Delta^2 + \frac{\Delta t}{\rho}\left(2\mu_{i+\frac 12}+ 2\mu_{i-\frac 12}+ \mu_{j+\frac12} + \mu_{j-\frac12}\right)},
\qquad (13.8)
$$
with the neighbour terms
$$
N_{ij} = 2\mu_{i+\frac 12}u_{i+1} + 2\mu_{i-\frac 12}u_{i-1}
 + \mu_{j+\frac12}\big(u_{j+1} + \Delta(\partial_x v)_{j+\frac12}\big)
 - \mu_{j-\frac12}\big(-u_{j-1} + \Delta(\partial_x v)_{j-\frac12}\big),
$$
where $r = \mathbf{u}^{\text{old}}$ is the right-hand side. The denominator is always
larger than $\Delta^2$, so the iteration is diagonally dominant and converges for
any $\Delta t$.

> **In the code: `viscosity.h`, `relax_viscosity()`**
>
> ~~~literatec
> w.x[] = (r.x[]*sq(Delta) + dt/rho[]*(2.*mu.x[1]*u.x[1] + 2.*mu.x[]*u.x[-1]
>          + mu.y[0,1]*(u.x[0,1] + (u.y[1,0] + ua.y[1,1])/4. - (u.y[-1,0] + ua.y[-1,1])/4.)
>          - mu.y[]*(- u.x[0,-1] + (ua.y[1,-1] + u.y[1,0])/4. - (ua.y[-1,-1] + u.y[-1,0])/4.)))
>        /(lambda.x*sq(Delta) + dt/rho[]*(2.*mu.x[1] + 2.*mu.x[] + mu.y[0,1] + mu.y[]));
> ~~~
>
> This is (13.8); `foreach_dimension()` produces the $y$
> and $z$ versions by rotating the indices. `lambda.x` is 1 except in
> axisymmetric runs.

## Why the pressure gradient is added and removed

In `centered.h`:

~~~literatec
correction (dt);       // u += dt*g    (g: centred pressure gradient + acceleration at t^n)
mgu = viscosity (u, mu, rho, dt, mgu.nrelax);
correction (-dt);      // u -= dt*g
~~~

The velocity coming out of advection does not contain the pressure gradient
or the body forces. Near a wall or the free surface that velocity can be far
from the final $\mathbf{u}^{n+1}$, and the implicit viscous operator, together with
the velocity boundary conditions applied during the solve, would act on the
wrong field. Adding $\Delta t\,\mathbf{g}^n$ first gives the solver a good
estimate of $\mathbf{u}^{n+1}$; removing it afterwards leaves room for the new
pressure gradient $\mathbf{g}^{n+1}$, which the projection adds at the end
of the step.

## Embedded boundaries

With `embed.h`, `centered.h` includes `viscosity-embed.h`
instead. Its operator is the same but adds, in cut cells, the viscous flux
through the piece of solid surface inside the cell, computed from the
Dirichlet condition $\mathbf{u}= 0$ on the solid with the gradient
reconstruction of [section 19.3](19_embed#sec:embed-dirichlet).

